WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:03.550
- So in the early debates
about the Constitution,

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:06.830
there are folks that wanted
a strong central leadership

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:07.730
and other folks who didn't,

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:10.130
because they felt it felt
a lot like George III.

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:14.540
How did the existence of
Washington as a person

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:15.930
affect the debate?

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:16.763
- It's amazing.

00:00:16.763 --> 00:00:19.100
So George Washington, his
friends have to plead with him

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.660
to come to the Constitutional Convention.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.760
He thinks, frankly,
that rewriting the rules

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:24.750
of the country, it's not gonna work.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.370
But they finally convince him to come

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:29.320
and they make him president
of the convention.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:31.350
And they put him at the front of the room,

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:34.660
and he says almost nothing
during the entire four months.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:38.090
But what he does is at the
front of the room he is a model,

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:40.460
because, remember, he
resigned his commission

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.970
as commander of the Continental
Army and gave up power.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:45.850
He didn't seize the power that he had as,

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:48.340
he was basically the biggest
celebrity in America,

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.340
and he gave his commission
back to the government,

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:54.130
which was an act of sublimating

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:55.410
his own personal self interest

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.320
for the benefit of the republic.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:00.530
And that's the model they
wanted for the Constitution.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:03.610
So he didn't say much, but
they designed basically,

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:05.970
as one writer said that what
they were essentially doing

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.140
was writing his future job description,

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:11.030
because everybody in the
room knew he would ultimately

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:12.140
become the president,

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.370
because there was nobody
else in America like him.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.700
And what was important was
not that he was a general,

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.990
but that he had this virtue inside of him,

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.220
which was that he would know how far to go

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.810
and when to stop, and when
to protect those liberties.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.640
And so he sat on a chair
with a sun on the back of it,

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.090
and at the end of the proceedings,

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:33.440
Benjamin Franklin, who was
the only other great, kind of,

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.370
superstar in America who
participated in the convention

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:39.650
said that he looked at that
sun on Washington's chair,

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.650
and he wasn't sure whether
it was a rising sun

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:42.550
or a setting sun,

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.400
but now after they finished their work,

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.050
he had decided that the sun was rising,

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:50.040
which was basically anointing
and blessing everything

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.410
that had happened there,

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:54.340
and this new office of
presidency that they had created,

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:56.410
that George Washington
was to go walk into,

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:58.410
and that's why Washington's statue

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.210
is in front of Independence Hall

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:03.420
where the Constitutional
Convention took place.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.820
But in the dialogue over four months,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.840
you almost never see his words.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.630
He was there as a symbol and participant,

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:14.440
but not like James
Madison, or James Wilson,

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.910
or Gouverneur Morris who were
in there in the nitty gritty

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:17.970
of every little detail.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.640
- Do we know what Washington was thinking,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.130
did he want the job, did he have a view,

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:24.207
or he just said,

00:02:24.207 --> 00:02:26.810
"Hey, I'm just gonna do
what everyone else decides"?

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.780
- Fortunately, they did
everything in secret.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:31.780
Washington was so virtuous
that he didn't even write

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:33.270
about it in his diary.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:36.550
Now, fortunately, we have other
people who did keep diaries,

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:39.337
and James Madison took notes and said,

00:02:39.337 --> 00:02:42.790
"Publish them only after
all 55 members are dead."

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:45.120
What Washington thought is he
wanted a central government,

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.230
because as a general he
knew there had to be an army

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:48.850
to handle rebellions,

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:51.210
and there had been Shays' Rebellion

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:53.980
that had tested under the
Articles of Confederation.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.580
So he wanted a strong government,

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:58.570
but he was, and he was very worried

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:01.030
that having been
successful revolutionaries,

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:03.810
they could do what he
knew was hard by history,

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:05.570
which is that revolutionaries
aren't very good

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:06.880
at creating governments.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.060
And so he knew that what they were doing

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.550
was a real risk and a real gamble.

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:14.470
He believed that it
could be done, however.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:17.600
He believed that a strong
national government was required.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.120
But then was he was given the job,

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.750
he was incredibly nervous,

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.980
this incredible military
leader basically thought

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:25.350
that he might fail.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:27.670
It was more likely than not
that this whole darn thing

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:29.499
would fail and that he might fail.

00:03:29.499 --> 00:03:32.060
And as he rode to his inauguration,

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.510
he kept writing letters and in his diary

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:36.850
talking about how the
expectations of his countrymen

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:38.450
were just too much for him.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:40.800
And that was both a personal worry,

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:42.560
and he also worried about monarchy.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:43.770
How does monarchy build?

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:46.580
It's either when a monarch demands power

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:49.920
or when the mob hands all
of the power to the person

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.540
and says, "Do everything for us,"

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:52.980
and gives them ultimate power.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:56.670
And what they knew in the
convention was that human beings

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.880
were sinful and could not handle power.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.950
If they were given the
power, they would abuse it

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:03.300
as surely as the sun
comes up in the morning.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:06.230
And he was worried that
if given too much power,

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:08.740
perhaps he could be susceptible to that.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:12.216
And so he was for as confident
and as much of a model

00:04:12.216 --> 00:04:13.049
of strength that he was,

00:04:13.049 --> 00:04:14.620
he was a very nervous guy.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.030
- And it sounds like an
unusual person where the power,

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:20.450
at least as the history I've read,

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:22.980
didn't corrupt him and he
didn't try to do a power grab.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.470
- That's right, Washington was constantly,

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:29.640
he was a man of a rigid code,

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.470
and he believed that the
standards were necessary

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:34.310
for the proper kind of human behavior.

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:37.480
He wrote a list of a
hundred different things

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:40.560
that a gentleman should
do to comport himself

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.290
in the proper way in society,

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.040
and that's what made
him such a good model.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:45.953
- I'd like a copy of that.

00:04:45.953 --> 00:04:46.960
- (laughs) Yes, exactly.
- I have a feeling

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:48.540
I don't check most of them off. (laughing)

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:49.890
- We'd all be better.

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:51.400
But things like clearing your throat,

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.370
how you behave in the presence of a lady.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:58.330
And he believed in these codes,

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:01.770
because he believed if
everybody maintained them,

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:03.390
then the system would work.

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.560
This was during the
period where Newton's laws

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:08.020
were making people think
about a clockwork universe

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.850
where if everything runs,

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:12.790
if the machine is put
together with tension, right,

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:15.330
so it recognized that
people were not angels,

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:16.330
tension in the machine,

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:18.390
but if everybody did their thing

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:21.560
and the pieces stayed in
their lane, as it were,

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:22.890
then the clock would work.

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:25.510
And so he had that code
which tried to keep him

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:28.470
in his place and keep
everybody else in his place,

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.070
and he set a standard,

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:31.610
and then everybody tried to live up to it,

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:33.517
as opposed to saying,
"Well, that standard's nice,

00:05:33.517 --> 00:05:35.130
"but I'm now gonna go do this."

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:37.980
And that's why when he resigned
his military commission,

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:41.400
and he also undid a coup that
some of his men were planning

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.250
back when he was leader of the army.

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:45.300
They were basically gonna
go to Congress and say,

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.040
this is in Newburgh, New York,

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.957
they were gonna go to Congress and say,

00:05:48.957 --> 00:05:50.167
"Unless you give us our money,

00:05:50.167 --> 00:05:51.560
"we're gonna stage a coup."

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:54.487
He found out about the plot,
went to his men and said,

00:05:54.487 --> 00:05:56.607
"This is a sin both against the revolution

00:05:56.607 --> 00:05:58.247
"and my own personal virtue,

00:05:58.247 --> 00:06:00.320
"because I put myself on the line."

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:02.138
They backed off.

00:06:02.138 --> 00:06:03.537
And what he coulda done is say,

00:06:03.537 --> 00:06:06.247
"Let's go, let's ride to
Washington and get you your money.

00:06:06.247 --> 00:06:07.827
"You fought in this war.

00:06:07.827 --> 00:06:10.527
"Your wives and children
are begging and poor.

00:06:10.527 --> 00:06:11.547
"You deserve this money.

00:06:11.547 --> 00:06:13.930
"Let's use our power and
authority and take it,"

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:15.250
and he said, "No."

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:17.250
Joseph Ellis, the
historian, writes about it

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:20.090
as being the last temptation
of Washington as a general.

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:22.220
He has the chance to grab the power,

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:24.230
and he says, "No, we shouldn't do it."

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:25.600
And that's why he was a model,

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:28.390
because he could resist the pull of power,

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:31.330
and that's the image in
which they wanted to create

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:32.330
this new government.

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:33.163
- Wow.

